# <%= it.scope %>/route-factory

Typed links across remotes: everything in
[`@mfeflow/route-factory`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@mfeflow/route-factory), plus the routes of this company
and one helper that makes links from them. `/react` is re-exported too. It is installed with
[`<%= it.scope %>/url-factory`](../url-factory), which `@mfeflow/route-factory` builds on, so both move together.
React is an optional peer dependency, needed only for `/react`.

## What to fill in

`src/routes.ts` holds the route definitions, one `defineRoutes(mount, routes)` per remote, where `mount` is the path
the shell serves the remote at. `account` is a placeholder; replace it with your remotes and list them in
`routeSets`.

`createCompanyLinks(config?)` returns `{ href, path }` over `routeSets`. A link id is `<remote>.<route>`, and a
missing param or an unknown route fails typecheck.

```ts
import { createCompanyLinks, routeSets, shellRoutes } from '<%= it.scope %>/route-factory';

const links = createCompanyLinks({ base: '/app' });
links.href('account.invoice', { params: { id: '42' } }); // /app/account/invoices/42

shellRoutes(routeSets); // what the shell needs to route to each remote
```

## Settings only, no state

`<%= it.scope %>/route-factory` is not a Module Federation singleton, so every remote gets its own copy of this
package. That is fine while it holds only route definitions and a function that builds links. Keep it that way: no
current route, listener or flag at module level here.
